@startuml
!include https://raw.githubusercontent.com/proveo-ca/identity/main/proveo.puml
title Runtime user boundary — never root, any host uid

' purpose: how an arbitrary run-as uid gets an identity and a writable HOME without
'          root; and why /home/agent is the REAL home while the harness name is
'          only an alias, plus the two build-time mistakes that reversal invites
' covers: packages/lib/entrypoint-lib.sh
' covers: internal/runner/**
' covers: internal/contract/home_symlink_test.go
' covers: defs/cecli/Dockerfile
' covers: defs/cursor/Dockerfile
' covers: defs/opencode/Dockerfile
' covers: defs/claudecode/mcp/Dockerfile
' covers: defs/claudecode/tests/test_workspace.sh
'
' ── WHAT ensure_runtime_user ACTUALLY DOES, IN TWO STEPS ────────────────────
' 1. SYNTHESIZE A PASSWD ENTRY, so getpwuid-based tooling does not choke on
'    "I have no name!". The line appended is
'    `agent:x:<uid>:<gid>:agent:<HOME>:/bin/bash`, and only when
'    `getent passwd <uid>` finds nothing AND /etc/passwd is writable — which is
'    why the step is best-effort rather than fatal.
' 2. GUARANTEE A WRITABLE HOME. The baked home is owned by the BUILD user and is
'    not writable by a different run-as uid until the deferred chmod lands, so an
'    empty or unwritable HOME falls back to /tmp.
'
' The same HOME guard also runs at SOURCE time, at the top level of the lib, not
' only inside the function — see the note below for why that placement is what
' makes ~/… seeding work in every entrypoint rather than just the fallback branch.
'
' ── WHY /home/agent AND NOT /home/<harness> ─────────────────────────────────
' sbx's built-in agent kits are authored against their own template, whose
' runtime user is `agent` with HOME=/home/agent: their setup.install chowns
' agent:agent, and the claude kit mounts FIVE block volumes AT
' /home/agent/.claude/{projects,sessions,todos,shell-snapshots,statsig}.
'
' So /home/agent must be a REAL directory and the actual home. An alias user plus
' a home SYMLINK is not enough, and that was the bug: a mount target cannot
' resolve through a symlink, so containerd materialises /home/agent as a fresh
' directory, SHADOWING the link. The volumes then land somewhere the agent never
' reads while $HOME points elsewhere — including `sessions`, which sbx's own kit
' calls load-bearing for `claude -c`. Measured: the stock image holds a session,
' and the same sbx invocation with a symlinked home died in 15s
' (e2e/ladder_test.go).
'
' The reversal is therefore: the REAL home is /home/agent, and the
' harness-specific name (/home/claude, /home/cursor, /home/opencode, /home/cecli)
' is the alias. /home/${USER_NAME} stays as a symlink because image healthchecks
' and def tests read paths through it, and nothing ever mounts there. All four
' harnesses do this; cecli and opencode joined cursor and claudecode because
' `docker: sbx` is now the only backend that hands them a daemon.
'
' ── THE SECOND MISTAKE THE REVERSAL INVITES: chown THROUGH THE ALIAS ────────
' `chown -R` given a SYMLINK as its argument chowns the link and stops — it does
' not recurse into the target without -L. `mkdir -p`, by contrast, follows the
' link happily. So a build step reads correctly and is wrong, and the two
' harnesses hit it differently:
'
'   opencode  mkdir -p /home/${USER_NAME}/.config/opencode  -> real home, ROOT-owned
'             chown -R … /home/${USER_NAME}                 -> the LINK only
'             The agent died at its first write:
'               mkdir: cannot create directory
'               '/home/agent/.config/opencode/agents': Permission denied
'             after a build that reported success, with `ls -l /home/opencode`
'             showing 1000:1000 because that IS the link.
'   cursor    `.cursor` was created root-owned through the link, so the seed's
'             `mkdir .cursor/agents` hit Permission denied and the entrypoint's
'             `set -e` killed the container before the agent ever ran. sbx
'             reported that as "failed to run sandbox container" with no cause.
'
' Both are now fixed by pointing the chown at /home/agent. claudecode chowns
' /home/${USER_NAME}/.claude — a subpath, which ends at a real directory — which
' is why opencode alone failed the detached smoke suite. Guarded by
' internal/contract TestNoDockerfileChownsTheHomeSymlink.

actor "Developer\nuid:gid" as DEV

node "Host" as HOST <<host>> {
  component "cmd/proveo run\ninternal/runner" as RUN <<app>>
  component "defs/*/run.sh\nthin shim → exec proveo" as SHIM <<app>>
  database "bind-mounted repo" as REPO <<db>>
}

node "Agent container" as CTR <<host>> {
  component "image USER (uid 1000)\nbaked non-root default" as IMGUSER <<app>>
  component "ensure_runtime_user\npasswd entry + writable HOME" as ENSURE <<app>>
  component "agent process" as AGENT <<app>>
  component "/home/agent — the REAL home\n/home/<harness> — an alias symlink" as HOMES <<app>>
  component "root / gosu / privilege drop" as ROOT <<error>>
}

DEV ARROW_MAIN SHIM
SHIM ARROW_MAIN RUN
RUN ARROW_MAIN CTR : " ""docker run --user $(id -u):$(id -g)"" \n""--cap-drop=ALL --security-opt=no-new-privileges""\n""--pids-limit"" "
IMGUSER ARROW_OPTIONAL AGENT : bare ""docker run"" (no wrapper)\nstill lands non-root
RUN ARROW_MAIN ENSURE
ENSURE ARROW_MAIN AGENT
AGENT ARROW_MAIN REPO : writes come back owned\nby the developer
CTR ARROW_ERROR ROOT : errorLabel(never used)

note top of ENSURE
  **"I have no name!" is the symptom.** Any tool
  that calls ""getpwuid"" chokes on a uid with no
  passwd entry, so one is APPENDED — possible
  only where ""/etc/passwd"" is writable — which
  is why the step is best-effort, never fatal.
end note

note left of ENSURE
  **And ""HOME=/tmp"" is the fallback, not a
  default.** The baked home is owned by the
  BUILD user and is not writable by a different
  run-as uid until the deferred chmod lands, so
  an unwritable HOME degrades, never fails.
end note

note bottom of ENSURE
  **HOME is made writable in the shell itself, at source time — not only
  inside the prelude.** ""proveo-entrypoint prep"" runs as a subprocess, so it
  can only set HOME for its own process. A shell whose run-as uid has no
  passwd entry therefore keeps ""HOME=/"", and every later
  ""mkdir \"$HOME/.<tool>\""" fails with the tell-tale ""//.cursor"" double
  slash. Sourcing the guard before the prelude is what makes ~/… seeding
  work for an arbitrary uid, in every entrypoint rather than just the
  bash-fallback branch.
end note


note right of ENSURE
  **The arbitrary-uid problem.** ""--user 501:20"" is a uid
  the image has never heard of: no passwd entry, no home,
  so tools that call ""getpwuid"" or write to ""$HOME"" fail.
  ""ensure_runtime_user"" (packages/lib/entrypoint-lib.sh)
  synthesizes an identity and a writable HOME **without
  root** — one generic helper, identical across harnesses.

  There is no gosu and no in-container privilege drop:
  the process starts unprivileged and stays there.
end note

note bottom of HOMES
  **""mkdir"" follows the alias; ""chown"" does not.** The harness-named
  home is a SYMLINK to ""/home/agent"" (sbx mounts its volumes at the
  real path, so that one has to be the directory). A build step then
  reads perfectly and is wrong:

    ""mkdir -p /home/${USER_NAME}/.config/opencode""     → real home, ROOT-owned
    ""chown -R ${USER_NAME}:${USER_NAME} /home/${USER_NAME}""  → the LINK only

  Nothing fails. ""ls -l /home/${USER_NAME}"" even reports the right
  owner, because that IS the link. The agent dies later, on its first
  write — ""mkdir: cannot create directory
  '/home/agent/.config/opencode/agents': Permission denied"" — one
  layer and one process away from the line that caused it.

  Measured on ""proveo/opencode:local"", 2026-09-05: ""\.config"",
  ""\.config/opencode"" and ""\.local/share/opencode"" all ""0:0"" beneath a
  ""/home/agent"" owned by ""1000:1000"". cursor chowns ""/home/agent"";
  claudecode chowns ""/home/${USER_NAME}/\.claude"", a subpath that ends
  at a real directory — which is why opencode alone failed the
  detached smoke suite. Guarded by ""internal/contract""
  ""TestNoDockerfileChownsTheHomeSymlink"".
end note

note top of HOMES
  **Five volumes decide the real home.**
  sbx's claude kit mounts blocks at
  /home/agent/.claude/{projects,sessions,…},
  and a mount target cannot resolve through a
  symlink — containerd shadows the link.
end note

note left of HOMES
  **The 15-second death.** ""sessions"" is what
  ""claude -c"" resumes from. With the volumes
  landing off-home, the stock image held a
  session and the same sbx invocation with a
  symlinked home died in 15s (ladder_test).
end note

note right of HOMES
  **Two harnesses, one root-owned tree.**
  cursor: the seed's ""mkdir .cursor/agents"" hit
  Permission denied, ""set -e"" killed the
  container, and sbx reported only 'failed to
  run sandbox container' with no cause.
end note

note bottom of REPO
  Running as the invoking uid is what keeps bind-mounted
  edits usable on the host — a root-owned artifact left
  behind by an agent is a support burden, not a sandbox.
end note
@enduml
